# Force LF line endings for files where byte-equivalence matters
# across platforms (Windows checkouts would otherwise autoconvert to
# CRLF and break Rust-side byte comparisons).
#
# `toolr-manifest.json` is serialised by
# `toolr_core::build_fragment::serialise_fragment` with literal `\n`
# line terminators, and `toolr self build-manifest --check` compares
# the on-disk file byte-for-byte against the regenerated output.
*.json text eol=lf

# `skills/*/references/*.md` are generated by
# `cargo xtask build-skill-refs` and byte-compared by `--check`.
# Same byte-equivalence story as the JSON snapshots above — Windows
# checkouts with `core.autocrlf=true` would otherwise rewrite the
# committed files to CRLF and fail the check.
skills/*/references/*.md text eol=lf

# `skills/*/examples/**/*.py` are read by the manifest snapshot test
# (`crates/toolr-core/tests/skill_authoring_examples.rs`). The toolr
# docstring parser preserves source newlines verbatim in the group
# `description` field, so a CRLF checkout on Windows produces a
# manifest whose JSON values contain `\r\n` and no longer match the
# LF-canonical committed snapshot.
skills/*/examples/**/*.py text eol=lf

# The rest of the generator's outputs: `docs/writing-commands/files/*.md`
# (`types::*_snippet`) and the packaging skill's copied `pyproject.toml`.
# The snippet sources beside them are LF too so the directory is uniform;
# xtask normalises CRLF on read either way.
docs/writing-commands/files/* text eol=lf
skills/*/examples/pyproject.toml text eol=lf
